  "meta": {
    "title": "ww — guess the middle, pour, compare",
    "blurb": "The copy language, with no separator. Push the first half on stack 1, guess where the middle is, pour stack 1 into stack 2 — which turns it right way up — and check the second half against it.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "aa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbabb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "a",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abba",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "mirrored, not copied"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbab",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "copy-language",
        "not-context-free",
        "two-stacks"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "One stack reverses whatever it stores, which is why it can check **wwᴿ** and not **ww**. Two stacks can reverse twice. Pouring stack 1 into stack 2 with ε-moves leaves the *first* symbol of w on top, which is the order the copy arrives in.\n\nThe pour happens once, at a guessed moment: every position in the word is tried as the middle, and only the right one can empty stack 2 exactly as the input runs out.\n\nTwo stacks are enough to simulate a Turing machine's tape — one holds the cells to the left of the head, the other the cells to the right — so this machine is as powerful as a machine gets. Compare **Perfect copy: w#w** among the queue automata, which needs the separator but no guess."
    }
  }
}
